WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
The first handling rule is simple: never store plaintext passwords.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:09.700
A password should be accepted only long enough to verify it, change it, or establish a new credential.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:20.000
It should not be written to application logs, analytics, telemetry, support tickets, debug dumps, crash reports, data warehouses, or administrative dashboards.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.940
Once a password lands in routine operational data, it becomes much harder to control.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.480
Passwords must be protected in transit.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:38.260
Login, registration, and reset flows should use HTTPS and should avoid exposing credentials in URLs, query strings, referrer data, or client-side logs.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.440
Password fields should be handled as sensitive data in both frontend and backend code.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:51.420
Even temporary convenience logging during development is dangerous because those logs are often copied forward into shared tools or retained longer than expected.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.880
Authentication error messages need care.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:59.940
A login response should help legitimate users without giving attackers a reliable account enumeration signal.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.280
Reset flows should be designed so that requesting a reset does not confirm whether an account exists.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.660
That does not mean every message must be vague to the point of being unusable.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:16.520
It means the product, support, and security teams should intentionally choose responses that balance usability and risk.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.060
Password verification should also be separated from authorization and session management.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.520
Verifying a password proves that the presented secret matches the stored verifier.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:35.740
It does not decide which resources the user can access, whether the account is locked, whether MFA is required, or whether existing sessions should continue after a password change.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.360
Keeping these concerns separate makes the system easier to reason about and test.
